1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,373
Why can you add numbers in any order, but
not subtract them in any order?

2
00:00:04,823 --> 00:00:09,578
The answer comes from a handful of
properties that every number follows. In

3
00:00:09,578 --> 00:00:13,698
this lesson, we'll meet them, and see why
they make algebra work.

4
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:18,716
First, the commutative property. Commute
means to move around.

5
00:00:19,166 --> 00:00:24,771
For addition, a plus b equals b plus a.
For example, three plus five and five plus

6
00:00:24,771 --> 00:00:26,206
three are both eight.

7
00:00:26,656 --> 00:00:30,176
For multiplication, a times b equals b
times a.

8
00:00:30,626 --> 00:00:36,510
But subtraction is not commutative. Five
minus three is two, while three minus five

9
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:37,645
is negative two.

10
00:00:38,767 --> 00:00:41,881
Next, the associative property. It's about
grouping.

11
00:00:42,331 --> 00:00:48,051
When you add three numbers, it doesn't
matter which pair you add first. a plus b,

12
00:00:48,051 --> 00:00:50,310
plus c, equals a, plus b plus c.

13
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:57,212
The same is true for multiplication. Two
times five, times seven, equals two, times

14
00:00:57,212 --> 00:00:59,933
five times seven. Both are seventy.

15
00:01:01,067 --> 00:01:05,419
The most important one in algebra is the
distributive property.

16
00:01:05,869 --> 00:01:12,126
a times the quantity b plus c equals a b
plus a c. The a gets multiplied by each

17
00:01:12,126 --> 00:01:14,317
term inside the parentheses.

18
00:01:14,767 --> 00:01:20,490
Think of a rectangle that's three wide,
and x plus four long. Its area is three x

19
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:21,337
plus twelve.

20
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:26,459
So three times the quantity x plus four
equals three x plus twelve.

21
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,440
The distributive property also makes
mental math easier.

22
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:37,545
Seven times ninety eight is the same as
seven times the quantity one hundred minus

23
00:01:37,545 --> 00:01:37,821
two.

24
00:01:38,271 --> 00:01:42,473
That's seven hundred minus fourteen, which
is six hundred eighty six.

25
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:45,776
Finally, identities and inverses.

26
00:01:46,226 --> 00:01:52,594
Adding zero leaves a number unchanged, so
zero is the additive identity. Multiplying

27
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:57,597
by one leaves it unchanged, so one is the
multiplicative identity.

28
00:01:58,047 --> 00:02:02,866
Every number has an opposite. a plus
negative a equals zero. That's the

29
00:02:02,866 --> 00:02:04,020
additive inverse.

30
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:09,857
And every number except zero has a
reciprocal. a times one over a equals one.

31
00:02:09,857 --> 00:02:12,235
That's the multiplicative inverse.

32
00:02:13,367 --> 00:02:16,119
Your turn. Name the property shown in each
line.

33
00:02:16,569 --> 00:02:23,259
Four times the quantity x minus two equals
four x minus eight. Six plus negative six

34
00:02:23,259 --> 00:02:29,710
equals zero. And two times five, times
seven, equals two, times five times seven.

35
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,781
Pause the video and decide.

36
00:02:32,231 --> 00:02:37,750
The first is the distributive property.
The second is the additive inverse. The

37
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:41,383
third is the associative property of
multiplication.

38
00:02:42,533 --> 00:02:48,104
If this helped, subscribe to School Math,
Explained, and turn on notifications so

39
00:02:48,104 --> 00:02:52,987
you don't miss the next lesson, on
combining like terms. See you there.

